<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rainilaiarivony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1828">1828</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rainilaiarivony was prime minister of the Merina kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> from 1864 to 1895 and its real ruler. He married three successive queens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasoherina">Rasoherina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-ii">Ranavalona II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-iii">Ranavalona III</a>, brought the court to Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and modernised the law, schools and army. After the French conquest of 1895 he was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rainilaiarivony.jpg" alt="Rainilaiarivony" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William John Edmonds · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Prime_Minister_Rainilaiarivony_of_Madagascar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rainilaiarivony was born on 30 January 1828 in Ilafy, one of the twelve sacred hills of Imerina. His father Rainiharo was a military officer and conservative adviser to Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-i">Ranavalona I</a>, prime minister from 1833 until his death in 1852 and the queen's consort. The family belonged to the Andafiavaratra clan of Ilafy, which with the Andrefandrova clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambohimanga">Ambohimanga</a> were the two most influential hova (commoner) families of the kingdom and held most political posts not given to the andriana (nobles).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After his father's death the queen promoted him and made him Royal Secretary and keeper of the Royal Seal. At 24 he led his first military expedition with his elder brother Rainivoninahitriniony against an uprising in the south-east. In May 1857 the brothers were drawn into a plot by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-francois-lambert">Joseph-François Lambert</a> and Prince Radama to overthrow Ranavalona; the plot was abandoned, and the brothers kept the queen's confidence. When she died in 1861 he held the palace guards in check and had Radama crowned before the soldiers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-ii">Radama II</a> made him Commander-in-Chief.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-ii">Radama II</a> was strangled on his brother's orders in May 1863, Rainilaiarivony proposed terms under which the sovereign needed the consent of the nobles to pass a death sentence or a new law. On 14 July 1864 Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasoherina">Rasoherina</a> deposed Rainivoninahitriniony, and Rainilaiarivony took his place and married her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In March 1876 he set up eight cabinet ministries and placed state envoys in the provinces to apply the law, collect taxes and report to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a>. About a third of the island remained outside Merina authority. Abroad he established embassies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, favoured an alliance with Britain, and agreed a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1867. He allowed foreigners to lease land for 99 years but not to buy it, and deliberately built no roads from the coast to the capital.</p><p>During the Second Franco-Hova War a French column bombarded the royal palace in September 1895, and he sought terms. Deposed by the French, he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> on 6 February 1896 and died there on 17 July. In 1900 his remains were brought back to the family tomb in Isotry, where General Gallieni spoke at the funeral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rainilaiarivony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later reforms under <b>Rainilaiarivony</b> introduced general conscription and hired foreign officers, but the army remained poorly paid and supplied.</blockquote><a href="/empire/merina-kingdom">Merina Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Queen Ranavalona III and her prime minister and husband <b>Rainilaiarivony</b> continued to govern through a modernised administration, a Protestant state church and an army.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-madagascar">French Protectorate of Madagascar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ilafy, in what is now <a href="/countries/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-17.88,48.41&amp;year=1872" aria-label="Ilafy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5863 2827 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rainilaiarivony"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6313 3097h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6313 3097h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-17.88,48.41&amp;year=1872">What was here in 1872? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
